WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.660
- [Narrator] This is the Camp Wildfire.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.330
On November 8th, 2018,
the fire tore across

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.473
a rural section of northern California.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.880
It was deadliest and most destructive fire

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.380
in California history.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.290
It consumed the town of
Paradise in a matter of hours,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.582
forcing its residents to flee in panic.

00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:21.314
(woman sobbing)

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:22.147
- [Woman] Please, God!

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:23.387
- [Woman] Please, God, just let us out!

00:00:23.387 --> 00:00:25.320
- [Woman] Let us get out safe!

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.160
- [Narrator] An investigation
found that the Camp Fire

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.080
was caused by a line

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.050
owned by Pacific Gas
and Electric, or PG&amp;E.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.370
It was the latest in a string of fires

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.010
that ultimately led the
state's largest utility company

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.810
to file for bankruptcy,
but with this bankruptcy,

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.840
it isn't just shareholders and creditors

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.610
who have an interest in
the future of the company.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.930
Millions of Californians
rely on the utility

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.960
to keep the lights on,
and some are questioning

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.100
whether PG&amp;E can be trusted to
do that safely and reliably.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:53.933
That's because,

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:56.210
according to documents reviewed
by the Wall Street Journal,

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.350
the company knew for years

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.790
its equipment had the
potential to start fires,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.070
yet repeatedly delayed
the necessary upgrades

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.240
to make some of its lines safer.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.163
This is the story of how
PG&amp;E filed for bankruptcy.

00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:11.020
(copier humming)
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.990
In 1905, newly-incorporated
Pacific Gas and Electric

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.340
was competing with other utilities

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.780
to electrify northern California

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.590
with power generated high
in the Sierra Nevada.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.440
- A vision of a regional power system

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.410
was something that
really hadn't been tried

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.580
in too many other places around the world.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.740
The predecessors to PG&amp;E
built a series of dams

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.680
then built long, high-voltage
transmission lines

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.963
across the Central
Valley to San Francisco.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.490
PG&amp;E emerged really through consolidation

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.600
of a number of companies to
serve more and more parts

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.133
of northern California with
increasing demands for energy.

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:51.979
♪ I wash and dry your
clothes, play your radios ♪

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:55.390
♪ I can heat your coffee pot ♪

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.830
Remember, just plug in, I'm Reddy!

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.710
- [Narrator] But safety
and reliability issues

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.430
have plagued the utility since the '90s.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.840
In 1996, PG&amp;E settled a
multi-million-dollar lawsuit

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.540
alleging it tainted drinking water

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.100
around Hinkley, California.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.520
In 2001, the California energy crisis

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.040
resulted in rolling blackouts

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.080
and drove the corporation's utility arm

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.170
to file for bankruptcy, and in 2010,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.960
a PG&amp;E gas pipeline exploded
in San Bruno, California,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.450
killing eight people.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.760
PG&amp;E was placed on federal probation

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.710
as a result of the explosion

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.920
to ensure the utility operated safely

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.593
in the communities it served,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.030
and many of those northern
California communities

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.770
were growing in rural areas.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.410
- A lot of the residential construction

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.330
that's occurred over
the last several decades

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.120
has been in what we would call
the Wildland-Urban Interface,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.770
the fringe of the built environment,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.200
putting people out into the forest.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.640
Since that time, there hasn't been a lot

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.430
of new construction of
the backbone system.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.640
- [Narrator] Many of the
utility's transmission towers

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.693
were constructed in the first
half of the 20th century.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.360
Outside of Paradise is a
high-voltage transmission line

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.050
owned by PG&amp;E.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.910
The 56-mile line, known
as the Caribou-Palermo,

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.860
was built in 1921.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.990
Its towers and lines tap
into a hydroelectric system

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.610
known as the Stairway of Power.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.200
PG&amp;E estimated the mean life expectancy

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.220
of its high-voltage transmission towers

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.970
was 65 years old, but the
company estimated the average age

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.310
of all the towers still in service was 68.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:35.133
The oldest towers in the
system were 108 years old.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.700
- Equipment like these
high-voltage and low-voltage lines

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.010
does fail, but the failure
is low-consequence,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.990
so you'd have an older transmission line

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.640
that was nearing the
end of its service life

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.580
and something would break
during a big winter storm

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.030
in the Sierra, and when the storm ended,

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.400
PG&amp;E crews could go out and fix the line.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.050
What's changed is when the
power lines fail in California

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.493
and the consequences of a failure.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.500
- [Narrator] From 2013 to 2019,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.280
a historic drought
swept across California,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.540
killing millions of trees

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.923
and greatly increasing the risk of fire.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.400
According to a PG&amp;E list

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.230
obtained by the Wall Street Journal,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.030
the Caribou-Palermo was one of the grid's

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:19.250
worst performing circuits.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.270
It also ran through areas
that were identified

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.453
as posing elevated and extreme fire risks.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.530
In 2017, PG&amp;E equipment started 18 fires

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.330
that killed 22 people, according
to state fire officials.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.280
According to an internal
presentation from that same year,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.730
the company said it needed a plan

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.550
to replace its steel
structure transmission towers

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.000
and better manage its equipment

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.711
to prevent it from causing fires.

00:04:42.711 --> 00:04:44.990
(quietly tense synth music)

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.610
On the morning of November 8th, 2018,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.770
winds picked up before
sunrise near Paradise.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.600
A hook connecting a power line

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.780
to a Caribou-Palermo tower failed,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.600
causing the line to strike the tower

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.030
and emit sparks that fell to the ground.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:04:59.870
A few minutes later,

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:03.310
a PG&amp;E worker spotted a
small fire near the tower.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.282
That fire grew quickly.

00:05:05.282 --> 00:05:09.540
- Oh, my God!
(muffled chatter)

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.373
- [Narrator] By eight a.m.,

00:05:10.373 --> 00:05:13.200
local officials had given
the order to evacuate.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.340
By 10:45, the fire had
overtaken parts of Paradise.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.240
- [Woman] Trying to get out of Paradise.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.113
This is bad.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:24.092
- [Man] Oh, my God!

00:05:24.092 --> 00:05:25.841
- [Officer] Ladder 13, it's bad!

00:05:25.841 --> 00:05:26.948
- [Man] Keep going!

00:05:26.948 --> 00:05:30.352
(tense synth music)

00:05:30.352 --> 00:05:35.352
(engine rumbling)
(tense synth music)

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.490
- [Narrator] By six p.m.,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.816
the fire had completely consumed the town.

00:05:40.816 --> 00:05:42.960
(dramatic synth music)

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.470
In the aftermath, the
local sheriff's office

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.940
said bodies were found in vehicles,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.023
most likely of people who were
trying to escape the fire.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.460
Anthropologists and a
forensic dentist were tasked

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.980
with identifying human remains

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.150
that sometimes consisted

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.479
only of a few bone fragments or teeth.

00:05:59.479 --> 00:06:01.640
(dramatic synth music)

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.220
For PG&amp;E, the destruction
caused by the Camp Fire

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:05.853
was an existential threat.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.100
The utility faced thousands
of wildfire claims

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.480
seeking damages for, amongst other things,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:15.680
wrongful death, personal
injury, and property damage.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.110
From October 2018 to January 2019,

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.480
PG&amp;E's market value slid

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.920
from about $25 billion to nine billion.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.120
By declaring bankruptcy,
the utility's strategy

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.970
was to fold these claims
into a bankruptcy proceeding,

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.060
allowing the company to
negotiate settlements

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.083
with all of its creditors,
including the fire victims.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:36.840
A Wall Street Journal investigation

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.080
revealed the company knew
that 57 of the steel towers

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.220
on the Caribou-Palermo line
needed hardware replaced

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.210
and another 49 towers needed
to be replace entirely

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.030
before the Camp Fire.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.250
In 2013, PG&amp;E told federal regulators

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.910
it had a $30 million
plan to replace equipment

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.660
on the Caribou-Palermo line,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:59.360
but it repeatedly delayed
the project for five years.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.840
It was slated to begin
as late as June 2018,

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:05.810
but it didn't happen.
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.930
The utility released a
statement in response

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:10.210
to the Journal's reporting,
saying the scheduled work

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.920
wasn't maintenance-related

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.720
and that the tower that
malfunctioned before the Camp Fire

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.610
wasn't slated to be part of the project.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.070
PG&amp;E later acknowledged in federal court

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.210
it had long known that
its high-voltage lines

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.160
could fail and trigger fires,
but said that such fires

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.143
have historically been relatively rare.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.810
- [Michael] I think the
utilities in California

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.010
recognize that they are operating

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.820
in a different world today

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:38.710
and that, if they cause fires,
they won't have a business.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:40.500
- [Narrator] In December 2019,

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.920
state investigators
found a dozen violations

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.180
the utility had committed

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.520
in maintaining its
transmission lines and towers.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.090
PG&amp;E acknowledges it still has work to do.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.690
Since the Camp Fire, it has
inspected all its towers,

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.630
lines, and substations.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.070
It identified 1200 immediate safety risks

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:00.740
and another 10,000 less urgent
repairs and is making fixes.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:02.650
It has also committed
to sharing the results

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.510
of its inspections with state
regulators and the public.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:09.220
PG&amp;E has also permanently
retired the Caribou-Palermo line.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.400
- Well, I think in the long run,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.750
all of the utilities need
to harden their grid.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.770
In the short run, and
maybe even the medium-term,

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.300
PG&amp;E is also going to be
turning off the power.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.530
- [Narrator] Since
2018, PG&amp;E has initiated

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.810
several weather-related power shut-offs

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:27.420
to prevent its equipment
from starting more fires,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.660
including one that cut power

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.370
to nearly 750,000 homes and businesses.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.620
PG&amp;E's future remains uncertain.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.690
The utility has reached a
$13.5 billion settlement

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.190
with fire victims but
faces an major hurdle

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.150
in appeasing the state of California,

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.600
which has raised the
specter of a public takeover

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.040
if PG&amp;E doesn't enact certain reforms.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.760
- If Pacific Gas and Electric is unable

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.950
to secure its own fate and future,

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:58.950
then the state will
prepare itself as back-up

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.680
for a scenario where we
do that job for them.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.800
- People expect that their power
is gonna be on all the time

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.010
and that the system will
not cause them to be afraid,

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.340
but that's kind of where we live right now

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.410
in northern California,
and until that changes,

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:18.820
I think there is gonna be push

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:21.530
for much greater oversight of a company.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.000
I think that's a challenging environment

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.630
for a utility to operate in,

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.408
particularly one that's trying
to get out of bankruptcy.

00:09:27.408 --> 00:09:31.075
(dramatic orchestral music)

